---
/**
 * /products/pro/spec — the printable Polaris Pro spec sheet.
 *
 * Content is the record in data/products/pro-spec.ts; these three blocks stay as
 * markup because every table row carries its own citation and several carry
 * [verify:] flags that initVerifyFlags and scripts/check-claims.py look for.
 */
import SpecSheet from "../../../layouts/SpecSheet.astro";
import HardwarePlatform from "../../../components/HardwarePlatform.astro";
import { PRO_SPEC } from "../../../data/products/pro-spec";
---

<SpecSheet {...PRO_SPEC}>
  <Fragment slot="upsell">
    <div class="g3band"><b>Pro continues Mersive Solstice Gen 3.</b> Same lineage: your rooms, workflows, and muscle memory carry forward, and a mixed Gen 3 and Polaris estate is fully supported while you move.</div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="spec-tables">
      <h3>Sharing &amp; video</h3>
      <div class="famtbl spx-t"><table>
        <tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Simultaneous shares</td><td class="y">Up to 10 live sources composited side by side, laid out automatically as sources join and leave. Pro composites up to 10; Essentials runs the same software with a ceiling of 5, set by memory on the smaller chassis rather than by licensing [Ruled by Damian Blazy 12 Aug 2026 - see src/data/rulings.ts]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sharing paths</td><td>Browser web-join (no app) &middot; native AirPlay, Miracast, Google Cast (LAN) &middot; Polaris app for iOS and Android &middot; HDMI input for wired ingest</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Protocols</td><td>WebRTC, AirPlay, Miracast, Google Cast</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Video codecs</td><td>H.264 encode and decode &middot; H.265 encode and decode &middot; VP8 decode &middot; AV1 decode &middot; JPEG/MJPEG decode &mdash; all required. VP9 decode is preferred rather than required. Hardware colour conversion: YUY2/YUYV [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Resolution</td><td>Dual 4K (3840 &times; 2160 @ 60Hz) &middot; HD (1920 &times; 1080 @ up to 60Hz) &middot; (1280 &times; 720 @ up to 60Hz)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Video pipeline</td><td>Decodes 2&times; 4K60 or 4&times; 1080p60 concurrently; the compositor imposes no cap at the layout layer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Content protection</td><td>HDCP 2.0, with a software HDCP toggle on the HDMI input</td></tr>
      </table></div>

      <h3>Compute</h3>
      <div class="famtbl spx-t"><table>
        <tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Chipset / SoC</td><td>MediaTek MT8395 (Genio 1200): ARM SoC with Mali GPU and on-die APU, OpenGL ES 3.0 / Vulkan. The Gen 4 Pod ships as part of Mersive Pro, and the MT8395 was independently identified in the May 2025 device assessment.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RAM &middot; storage</td><td>Requirement: 16 GB LPDDR4x or better &middot; 32 GB eMMC or more [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]. As built: 16 GB LPDDR4 &middot; 32 GB flash &mdash; the capacities were observed directly in the May 2025 third-party device assessment, which identified Micron DDR4 parts and a 32 GB flash package; [confirmed by Damian Blazy 13 Aug 2026: 16 GB LPDDR4 and 32 GB flash are proven]</td></tr>
      </table></div>

      <h3>Network &amp; security</h3>
      <div class="famtbl spx-t"><table>
        <tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Ethernet</td><td>1&times; 10/100/1000 RJ-45, PoE+ (802.3at Type 2) [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wi-Fi</td><td>MediaTek MT7921: Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax), 802.11 a/b/g/n/ac/ax compliant, 2.4 + 5 GHz, MIMO 2&times;2; Bluetooth (MediaTek MT7921)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dual-network posture</td><td>Wired GbE and the wireless radio run simultaneously, kept isolated: corporate on the wire, guests on Wi-Fi</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Network protocols</td><td>TCP/IP, HTTP, HTTPS, SNTP/NTP, SSL, SSH, UDP &mdash; as published on the data sheet. These are the protocols the device speaks, not open listeners: the device exposes no TLS listener of its own</td></tr>
        <tr><td>802.1X authentication</td><td>&#10003; EAP-TLS, PEAP, TTLS or EAP-PWD, on wired Ethernet and on Wi-Fi (WPA2-Enterprise). Supply a CA certificate, a client certificate and a private key; the supplied private key is held encrypted at rest under a key that is itself sealed in the NXP SE050 secure element, rather than in plaintext configuration [verify: approved by Damian Blazy 13 Aug 2026 from the verification document. Engineering to confirm the wrapping mechanism end to end, since the document's own evidence elsewhere describes the 802.1X password as a NetworkManager system secret - engineering and CISO]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cross-network sharing</td><td>Cloud-negotiated direct connections; signaling outbound-only TLS on TCP 443; no inbound firewall rules; no VPN</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Device security</td><td>Mandatory secure boot &middot; NXP SE050 secure element holding the device&rsquo;s own identity keys &middot; signed updates with downgrade protection &middot; A/B firmware partitions with automatic rollback on a failed load &middot; no content persistence &middot; USB access by allowlist &middot; Kensington Nano Security Slot</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p class="note">Plain language: 802.1X means the network demands the device prove who it is before granting a port, and EAP-TLS proves it with a certificate instead of a password. Dual-network posture means the device sits on two networks at once, kept isolated, so guests share without ever touching the corporate LAN.</p>

      <h3>Physical &amp; environmental</h3>
      <div class="famtbl spx-t"><table>
        <tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Rear panel</td><td>Kensington Nano Security Slot &middot; Ethernet (PoE+) &middot; DC power &middot; analogue audio out &middot; 2&times; USB 3.0 Type-C (female), host mode &middot; 2&times; HDMI 2.0 out &middot; 1&times; HDMI 2.0 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USB</td><td>2&times; USB 3.0+ Type-C (female), host mode &middot; output 5 V at 0.9 A maximum &middot; no USB Power Delivery [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] [Ruled by Damian Blazy 26 Aug 2026, superseding the 13 Aug 2026 ruling: the connector is Type-C, not USB-A]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audio</td><td>Dedicated analogue audio-out jack: room audio goes straight to a soundbar or DSP without passing through the display</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Power</td><td>PoE+ (802.3at Type 2) for single-cable install, or 12 VDC, 2 A, 24 W max from the barrel jack. <b>The power supply is sold separately</b> on Pro &mdash; it is in the box on Essentials, because a Pro room is usually powered over Ethernet and a second brick would be waste [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dimensions &middot; weight</td><td>86 &times; 184 &times; 30 mm [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] &middot; [weight unverified - hardware team]. Pro and Essentials are <b>different chassis</b>: Essentials is 58 &times; 140 &times; 30 mm [Ruled by Damian Blazy 26 Aug 2026]. Both released data sheets print 150 &times; 67 &times; 30 mm and 204 g for both products, which cannot be true of two different enclosures; the sheets are being reissued [verify: mounting options and VESA/bracket accessories - product marketing]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Operating environment</td><td>Indoor use only &middot; 0&deg;C (32&deg;F) to 35&deg;C (95&deg;F) &middot; 30% to 70% RH ambient [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Storage environment</td><td>-20&deg;C (-4&deg;F) to 65&deg;C (149&deg;F) &middot; 10% to 90% RH [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drop durability</td><td>1 m onto concrete along any critical axis &mdash; face, corner or edge &mdash; with no cables connected [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ESD</td><td>IEC 61000-4-2 Level 4 &middot; &plusmn;8 kV contact &middot; &plusmn;15 kV air discharge [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Thermal</td><td>Fanless design strongly preferred. No throttling of CPU, GPU or VPU at 100% load, with LEDs at 100% and all other systems at 100%, sustained for not less than 8 hours [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Accessories</td><td>Power Supply Unit (not included) &middot; HDMI cable (not included)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regulatory</td><td>Required for launch: FCC (USA) &middot; UL (USA/Canada) &middot; CB Scheme / IECEE (international) &middot; UKCA (UK) &middot; CE (EU) &middot; RoHS (EU) &middot; SCIP (EU) [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] [verify: IC (Innovation Canada) was published here but appears in neither hardware requirement document - regulatory and legal] [verify: no TAA or NDAA statement appears on either released data sheet - legal and supply chain]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TAA</td><td>TAA compliant, required [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]. Any radio or SoC substitution triggers regulatory re-filing and a fresh country-of-origin attestation &mdash; see <a href="#platform-lifecycle">the 10-year platform</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warranty</td><td>Full warranty while on active subscription &mdash; no separate care contract, no support tier to renew [Ruled by Damian Blazy 12 Aug 2026, superseding the &ldquo;Limited 3-year manufacturer&rsquo;s warranty&rdquo; on the released data sheets, which is dated and to be reissued. See src/data/rulings.ts]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Three horizons, kept apart</td><td>These are three different things and read as a contradiction when they sit unlabelled beside each other. <b>Manufacturing-defect warranty:</b> 1 year [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]. <b>Commercial warranty:</b> runs with the subscription, and is capped at five years on a perpetual licence [Ruled by Damian Blazy 13 Aug 2026]. <b>Platform lifecycle:</b> 10 years [Ruling A, Damian Blazy, 26 Aug 2026 &mdash; <a href="#platform-lifecycle">the invariants</a>]. [verify: the five-year cap on a perpetual licence predates the 10-year platform lifecycle and now sits oddly beneath it. Flagged for CEO review; no commercial term is changed here - Damian Blazy]</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Licensing</td><td>3-year, 5-year or perpetual; managed in Polaris Cloud. On subscription the warranty runs as long as the room is subscribed; on a perpetual licence it is capped at 5 years [Ruled by Damian Blazy 13 Aug 2026]</td></tr>
      </table></div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="tier-table">
      <div class="famtbl spx-t"><table>
        <tr><th style="width:44%"></th><th>Polaris Essentials</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Simultaneous shares in the workspace</td><td>Up to 5</td><td class="y">Up to 10</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HDMI output</td><td>1&times; HDMI 2.0 out &middot; 4K (4096 &times; 2160) at 30&nbsp;Hz</td><td class="y">2&times; HDMI 2.0 out &middot; dual 4K (3840 &times; 2160) at 60&nbsp;Hz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HDMI input (wired ingest)</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="y">1&times; HDMI 2.0 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>True wireless BYOM conferencing</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="y">Included</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USB for room peripherals</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="y">2&times; USB 3.0 Type-C, host mode</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audio out (to soundbar / DSP)</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="y">Included</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Power over Ethernet</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="y">PoE+</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Power supply unit</td><td>In the box</td><td>Sold separately; PoE+ is typical</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Web-join, workspace, cross-network, Polaris Cloud</td><td class="y">Included</td><td class="y">Included</td></tr>
      </table></div>

      {/* The hardware platform block renders IN FULL here and nowhere else. Every
          other surface links to #platform-lifecycle instead of restating it. */}
      <HardwarePlatform as="h3" />
  </Fragment>
</SpecSheet>
